<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The calendar app's mark: plMail's own logo language, saying "calendar".

  It has to do two things at once. At launcher size, next to the mail icon on
  the same home screen, it has to be told apart at a glance; up close it has to
  read as the same family rather than as a second app that happens to be
  installed. So nothing here is borrowed from a generic calendar glyph. The
  stroke width, the round caps and joins, the palette and the paper behind it
  are all exactly ic_launcher_foreground's, and only the geometry is new: a page
  with two binder rings, a header rule and a row of date dots.

  THE COORDINATE SYSTEM is ic_launcher_foreground's, deliberately, so the two
  icons sit at the same visual weight and the same size under the launcher's
  mask. The art is drawn in a 48 unit square and the group scales it by 1.2 and
  translates by (18.6, 25.2), which maps the design origin (29.5, 24) onto the
  centre of the 108 canvas. A stroke of 5.5 here therefore paints 6.6 canvas
  units, which is what the mail mark paints.

  THE SAFE CIRCLE, and the arithmetic that must be redone rather than nudged if
  this art is ever rescaled. Only a circle of radius 33 about the canvas centre
  survives every launcher mask. Divided by the group's scale that is 27.5 design
  units about (29.5, 24), and half a stroke has to come off it, so every painted
  centreline must sit within 24.75 of (29.5, 24). The furthest ones are the
  page's bottom corners at (11.5, 39) and (47.5, 39), 23.4 out, and the tops of
  the two rings at (21, 4.5) and (38, 4.5), 23.8 out. There is about a unit in
  hand and no more.

  THE COLOURS are drawn from the mail mark's ramp — LogoStyle::Berry, the
  web's default — rather than from Google's brand hexes, which is what both
  icons used to wear and what the web deliberately dropped. Four of the seven,
  assigned so that no single one carries the whole shape: the page takes the
  deepest violet (#6d28d9), the header rule and the date dots the middle
  magenta (#c026d3), and the two rings the warm end (#db2777, #f43f5e).

  The old palette had one member that could not be used on a mark this thin —
  its yellow measured 1.62:1 against the off-white behind it, which is a 4dp
  stroke that is simply not there. This ramp has no such member: against
  #FAF9F7 the four measure 6.75, 4.48, 4.37 and 3.49 to 1, so even the lightest
  clears the 3:1 that a graphical object needs. The weakest is deliberately on
  a ring rather than on the page outline, which is the shape you recognise
  first.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <group
        android:scaleX="1.2"
        android:scaleY="1.2"
        android:translateX="18.6"
        android:translateY="25.2">

        <!-- The page. One closed path, so the corners join rather than meet. -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M17,10 L42,10 Q47.5,10 47.5,15.5 L47.5,33.5 Q47.5,39 42,39 L17,39 Q11.5,39 11.5,33.5 L11.5,15.5 Q11.5,10 17,10 Z"
            android:strokeColor="#6d28d9"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <!--
          The header rule, ending on the page's own centrelines so its round
          caps close against the frame instead of stopping short of it.
        -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M14.25,18.5 L44.75,18.5"
            android:strokeColor="#c026d3"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <!--
          The binder rings. They start on the top edge's centreline and run up,
          so what shows above the page is one stroke width of each: at launcher
          size two nubs, which is what a wall calendar looks like from across a
          room.
        -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M21,4.5 L21,10"
            android:strokeColor="#db2777"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M38,4.5 L38,10"
            android:strokeColor="#f43f5e"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <!--
          Three dates. Round caps on near zero length segments rather than
          circles, so they carry the same edge as everything else. Not a real
          grid: at 48dp a grid is a smudge, and three marks in a row is the most
          a page this size can say and still be read.
        -->
        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M21,29 L21.01,29"
            android:strokeColor="#c026d3"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M29.5,29 L29.51,29"
            android:strokeColor="#c026d3"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

        <path
            android:fillColor="#00000000"
            android:pathData="M38,29 L38.01,29"
            android:strokeColor="#c026d3"
            android:strokeLineCap="round"
            android:strokeLineJoin="round"
            android:strokeWidth="5.5" />

    </group>

</vector>
